# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'John'
TESTAMENT = 'New Testament'
GENRE = 'gospels'
SUBTITLE = 'The Word Made Flesh, the Signs and Witness, and the Life Given to Those Who Believe'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'John begins “In the beginning was the Word,” and says that “the Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us” (John 1:1, 14). The Gospel records John the Baptist’s witness that the Lord was to “be made manifest to Israel,” the signs he did, the feasts at Jerusalem, the raising of Lazarus, the King’s entry, the upper room, the cross and the empty tomb, and it states its purpose: “these are written, that ye might believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God” (John 1:31; 2:11; 11:25; 12:13; 20:31).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'The portions of John treated here belong to the Prophecy Program: the Word came to his own, the Baptist was sent that the Lord should be made manifest to Israel, and Nathanael confesses him the King of Israel (John 1:11, 31, 49). Its words on love, truth and worship are also placed in the Universal Truths Table.')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of John', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — The Word (Ch. 1)'), ('p', 'The Word who was with God and was God is made flesh, and the Baptist bears witness that the Lord should be made manifest to Israel (John 1:1, 14, 29, 31).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — Signs and Witness (Chs. 2–4)'), ('p', 'The beginning of miracles in Cana, the conversation about worshipping in spirit and truth, and the word that salvation is of the Jews (John 2:11; 4:22, 24).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — The Feasts and the Conflict (Chs. 5–10)'), ('p', 'The Lord bids them search the scriptures, calls himself the bread of life and the good shepherd, and is received by some and refused by others (John 5:39; 6:14, 35; 10:11).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — Lazarus and the King’s Entry (Chs. 11–12)'), ('p', 'The Lord is the resurrection and the life, and the crowd cries, “Blessed is the King of Israel” (John 11:25; 12:13, 15).'), ('h3', 'Movement 5 — The Upper Room and the Prayer (Chs. 13–17)'), ('p', 'The new commandment to love one another, the Lord’s own words in chapter 14 and the prayer that the disciples be sanctified through the truth (John 13:34; 14:6; 15:12; 17:17).'), ('h3', 'Movement 6 — The Cross, the Tomb and the Purpose (Chs. 18–21)'), ('p', 'The kingdom not of this world, the title on the cross, the signs written that the reader might believe, and the testimony that is true (John 18:36; 19:19; 20:30–31; 21:24).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in John', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how John should be understood within Israel’s prophetic program. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants readers to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Word</b> — “In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God” (John 1:1).', '<b>His Own</b> — “He came unto his own, and his own received him not” (John 1:11).', '<b>Manifest to Israel</b> — “That he should be made manifest to Israel, therefore am I come baptizing with water” (John 1:31).', '<b>Signs</b> — “This beginning of miracles did Jesus in Cana of Galilee, and manifested forth his glory” (John 2:11).', '<b>Salvation of the Jews</b> — “For salvation is of the Jews” (John 4:22).', '<b>Worship in Spirit and Truth</b> — “God is a Spirit: and they that worship him must worship him in spirit and in truth” (John 4:24).', '<b>Written That You Might Believe</b> — “These are written, that ye might believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God” (John 20:31).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in John', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — who He is, what He has done, and how He is presented. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered focus before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these verses together, you get a clear picture of how John presents Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Lamb of God</b> — “Behold the Lamb of God, which taketh away the sin of the world” (John 1:29).', '<b>The King of Israel</b> — “Thou art the Son of God; thou art the King of Israel” (John 1:49).', '<b>The Bread of Life</b> — “I am the bread of life: he that cometh to me shall never hunger” (John 6:35).', '<b>The Good Shepherd</b> — “I am the good shepherd: the good shepherd giveth his life for the sheep” (John 10:11).', '<b>The Resurrection and the Life</b> — “I am the resurrection, and the life” (John 11:25).', '<b>The Way, the Truth, and the Life</b> — “I am the way, the truth, and the life: no man cometh unto the Father, but by me” (John 14:6), reported as written, in the upper room with the disciples.', '<b>The Title on the Cross</b> — “JESUS OF NAZARETH THE KING OF THE JEWS” (John 19:19).'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in John', [('p', 'This section helps you see how John speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. John operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read John in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'John reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['the King of Israel confessed by Nathanael and by the crowd (John 1:49; 12:13)', 'the Lord made manifest to Israel (John 1:31)', 'his own who received him not (John 1:11)', 'salvation of the Jews (John 4:22)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in John that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how John contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['His Own — “He came unto his own, and his own received him not.” (John 1:11).', 'Manifest to Israel — “that he should be made manifest to Israel.” (John 1:31).', 'King of Israel — “thou art the King of Israel.” (John 1:49).', 'Salvation of the Jews — “salvation is of the Jews.” (John 4:22).', 'That Prophet — “This is of a truth that prophet that should come into the world.” (John 6:14).', 'Hosanna — “Blessed is the King of Israel that cometh in the name of the Lord.” (John 12:13).', 'Daughter of Sion — “Fear not, daughter of Sion: behold, thy King cometh.” (John 12:15).', 'The Title — “JESUS OF NAZARETH THE KING OF THE JEWS.” (John 19:19).', 'Why These Are Written — “these are written, that ye might believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God.” (John 20:31).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What John Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in John and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on the prophetic setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what John teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in John', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['His Own', '“He came unto his own, and his own received him not.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'John 1:11'], ['Manifest to Israel', '“that he should be made manifest to Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'John 1:31'], ['King of Israel', '“thou art the King of Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'John 1:49'], ['Salvation of the Jews', '“salvation is of the Jews.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'John 4:22'], ['That Prophet', '“This is of a truth that prophet that should come into the world.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'John 6:14'], ['Hosanna', '“Blessed is the King of Israel that cometh in the name of the Lord.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'John 12:13'], ['Daughter of Sion', '“Fear not, daughter of Sion: behold, thy King cometh.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'John 12:15'], ['The Title', '“JESUS OF NAZARETH THE KING OF THE JEWS.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'John 19:19'], ['Why These Are Written', '“these are written, that ye might believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'John 20:31']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in John that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what John teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['In the Beginning', '“All things were made by him; and without him was not any thing made that was made.”', 'John 1:3'], ['God’s Love for the World', '“For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son.”', 'John 3:16'], ['Worship in Spirit and Truth', '“God is a Spirit: and they that worship him must worship him in spirit and in truth.”', 'John 4:24'], ['The Truth Frees', '“ye shall know the truth, and the truth shall make you free.”', 'John 8:32'], ['Love One Another', '“A new commandment I give unto you, That ye love one another.”', 'John 13:34'], ['Greater Love', '“Greater love hath no man than this, that a man lay down his life for his friends.”', 'John 15:13'], ['The Word Is Truth', '“Sanctify them through thy truth: thy word is truth.”', 'John 17:17']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of John without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s prophetic story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing Israel-program commands for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to John in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Worship in spirit and truth</b> — God is a Spirit (John 4:24).', '<b>Seek the truth</b> — The truth shall make you free (John 8:32).', '<b>Love one another</b> — As the Lord loved (John 13:34).', '<b>Lay down your life for your friends</b> — Greater love hath no man (John 15:13).', '<b>Hold to the Word</b> — Thy word is truth (John 17:17).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for John', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach John in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['The Word (Ch. 1) — The Word made flesh. <i>(John 1:1, 11, 14, 29, 31)</i>', 'Signs and Witness (Chs. 2–4) — Spirit and truth. <i>(John 2:11; 4:22, 24)</i>', 'The Feasts and the Conflict (Chs. 5–10) — Bread of life and good shepherd. <i>(John 5:39; 6:14, 35; 10:11)</i>', 'Lazarus and the King’s Entry (Chs. 11–12) — The resurrection and the life. <i>(John 11:25; 12:13, 15)</i>', 'The Upper Room and the Prayer (Chs. 13–17) — Love one another. <i>(John 13:34; 14:6; 15:12–13; 17:17)</i>', 'The Cross, the Tomb and the Purpose (Chs. 18–21) — Written that ye might believe. <i>(John 18:36; 19:19; 20:30–31; 21:24)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read John, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward truth, love, and worship in spirit and truth without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching John simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, John stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]

# Place in God's Storyline (Master Standard v1.155): last item of Section 1
from storyline import S as _STORY
SECTIONS[0][2].append(('p', '<b>Place in God’s Storyline.</b> ' + _STORY['john'][1]))
